maya: FYI — saw a customer hit duplicated stream tokens after a 429 during a long-lived stream. They reported repeated partial content, looked like the client retried the whole stream after an auth refresh.

simon: Oof. Any repro or logs?

maya: Repro pretty minimal: start a streaming generation, proxy returns HTTP 429 mid-stream, client triggers auth refresh and then retries the request from start. Logs: `chunk seq=10 status=partial`, then `token refresh ok`, then `client retry id=stream-77 resume=false`, then we observe seq 10..end repeated.

niko: That matches an anti-pattern — blind replay for streaming endpoints. Two areas to fix: client-side defaults + server aid (cursor/confirm semantics).

niko: Suggestion for backoff: exponential with randomized multiplier to spread retries. Pseudocode:
```
const baseMs = isStreaming ? 500 : 100
for (let attempt=0; attempt<max; attempt++) {
  const jitter = 0.7 + Math.random() * 0.6 // 0.7..1.3
  await sleep(baseMs * Math.pow(2, attempt) * jitter)
}
```
Make streaming base higher to avoid thundering on partial reconnects.

kevin: From SDK side: introduce two headers and a client contract. `X-Resume-Token: <token>` (server emits after safe commit points) and `X-Replay-Allowed: true|false`. SDK should treat `X-Replay-Allowed:false` as non-idempotent — do not retry automatically; bubble 429 up.

kevin: Add SDK primitives: `client.createStream({idempotencyKey?, resumeTokenStore?})`. The store keeps the last commit token. On reconnect, client sends `X-Resume-Token` so server truncates already-sent content.

maya: How do we handle legacy models that don't emit resume tokens?

kevin: Opt-in rollout. Default behavior: if no resume token present, SDK defaults to conservative mode: no automatic retry on 429 for streaming. We can surface a clear error that suggests the app can retry manually.

niko: Also add a small server-side header `X-Commit-Interval-ms` to indicate commit granularity so clients know how frequently tokens are safe to persist.

simon: Nice. We should also add telemetry: `streaming.replay_attempt` and `streaming.duplicate_emitted` with tags (model, channel, replay_allowed). That will let us measure incidence and tune defaults.

maya: I'll write a minimal sample app for onboarding that demonstrates both: the naive pattern (what caused the bug) and the recommended pattern with resume tokens + conservative retry. The sample will include a failing-case log so folks see symptoms.

maya: Sample JS snippet for resume-aware loop (very small):
```
let resume = null
const reqHeaders = {}
if (resume) reqHeaders['X-Resume-Token'] = resume
try {
  const res = await fetch('/v1/stream', {method:'POST', headers: reqHeaders})
  for await (const chunk of res.body) {
    // chunk.payload, chunk.resumeToken
    process(chunk.payload)
    if (chunk.resumeToken) resume = chunk.resumeToken
  }
} catch (err) {
  if (err.status === 429 && res?.headers?.get('X-Replay-Allowed') === 'true') {
    await retryWithBackoff()
  } else {
    throw err
  }
}
```

kevin: We'll need an SDK unit/integration test: simulate mid-stream 429 + token refresh and ensure no duplicate token emits when `X-Resume-Token` is present; assert that client resumes rather than replays. Also test that when `X-Replay-Allowed:false` the SDK surfaces the error and does not retry.

